---
import CaseStudy from '../../layouts/CaseStudy.astro';
import Section from '../../components/Section.astro';
import FactList from '../../components/FactList.astro';
import Figure from '../../components/Figure.astro';
import Diagram from '../../components/Diagram.astro';
import SsfArchitecture from '../../components/diagrams/SsfArchitecture.astro';
import SsfOrderStates from '../../components/diagrams/SsfOrderStates.astro';
import listing from '../../assets/ryustorefront/listing-photos-desktop.png';
import pdp from '../../assets/ryustorefront/pdp-photos-desktop.png';
import pdpPhone from '../../assets/ryustorefront/pdp-photos-phone.png';
import checkout from '../../assets/ryustorefront/checkout-fixture-desktop.png';

const toc: [string, string][] = [
  ['problem', 'The problem'],
  ['built', 'What I built'],
  ['architecture', 'Architecture'],
  ['challenges', 'Hardest challenges'],
  ['evidence', 'Evidence'],
  ['limits', 'Tradeoffs and limits'],
  ['next', 'What I would improve'],
  ['links', 'Links'],
];
---
<CaseStudy
  title="RyuStoreFront: payments and inventory case study | Joshua Abrogena"
  description="A Java and Spring Boot shoe store with idempotent Stripe webhooks, atomic stock reservation, an order state machine and a transactional outbox."
  kind="Backend and payments"
  name="RyuStoreFront"
  status="Live at ryustorefront.com on AWS, with Stripe in test mode only (no card is charged). Earlier AWS sessions on 2026-11-15 and 2026-11-23 were measured and then destroyed; no number below comes from the current deployment. Order history and the admin API are still being built."
  stack="Java 25, Spring Boot 4, PostgreSQL 18, Next.js 16, Stripe (test mode), Terraform, AWS (ECS Fargate, RDS, ALB)"
  meta={[
    ['Kind', 'Solo portfolio project. No users, no real payments, no revenue.'],
    ['Measured where', 'Local: one dev VM. AWS: us-west-2. Every number says which.'],
  ]}
  toc={toc}
>
  <Section n={1} id="problem" title="The problem">
    <p>Online stores fail in the same few places. A payment arrives twice, or late, or after the cart expired. Two shoppers take the last pair. A background job dies halfway through. Most store demos show a catalogue and a cart and skip all of that.</p>
    <p>I wanted a small shoe store where those cases are designed, tested and written down. The scope is deliberately narrow: Stripe test mode only, flat $8 shipping, one currency, no tax engine, no multi-vendor features. Nothing here ships a real shoe.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section n={2} id="built" title="What I built">
    <p>What exists, and what does not. Nothing in the second column is claimed as done.</p>
    <div class="twocol">
      <div><h3>Built and tested</h3>
<ul>
                <li>Catalogue API with Postgres full-text search, filters, sort and pagination</li>
                <li>Sign-in with server-side sessions and CSRF tokens; a guest cart that merges on sign-in</li>
                <li>Checkout with atomic stock reservation, an idempotency key and an expiring hold</li>
                <li>Stripe Checkout (hosted page), a signed webhook, refunds when the money cannot be honoured</li>
                <li>Order state machine, and a transactional outbox with a background worker for email, cart clearing and expiry</li>
                <li>Next.js storefront wired to the API, with 142 demo products and 188 licensed photos served as AVIF and WebP</li>
                <li>Terraform for a persistent foundation stage and a destroyable per-session stage that runs the API, worker and frontend</li>
              </ul></div>
      <div><h3>Not built yet</h3>
<ul>
                <li>Order history and the admin API (the admin screens exist only as designs on fixtures)</li>
                <li>Login rate limiting, password reset, email verification</li>
                <li>A load test and a measured cloud cost</li>
                <li>Photos on the cart and order pages (their APIs return none yet)</li>
              </ul></div>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section n={3} id="architecture" title="System architecture">
    <Diagram id="ssf-arch" label="System architecture" description="The shopper's browser talks to a Next.js app, which proxies API calls to a Spring Boot API. The API and a worker share one PostgreSQL database. Stripe's hosted page takes the payment and sends a signed webhook to the API. The worker sends email.">
      <SsfArchitecture />
    </Diagram>
    <p>It is a modular monolith: packages for auth, cart, catalogue, checkout, inventory, orders, outbox, payments, notifications and a worker, with a Spring Modulith test that fails the build if a module reaches into another's internals. One jar runs as the API or, with a different profile, as the worker. PostgreSQL is the only store, so there is no cache or queue to keep consistent with it.</p>
    <Diagram id="ssf-states" label="Order state machine" description="Pending moves to paid (on Stripe's webhook) or cancelled. Paid moves to fulfilled or refunded. Fulfilled moves to shipped or refunded. Shipped moves to refunded. Cancelled and refunded are final.">
      <SsfOrderStates />
    </Diagram>
    <p>Every status change goes through one function that locks the order row, checks this table and records history. Asking for the status an order already has is a no-op, which is exactly what a replayed webhook looks like.</p>

    <h3>Decisions and what each cost</h3>
    <div class="tablewrap" role="region" tabindex="0" aria-label="Table: From the project's decision log">
      <table>
        <caption>From the project's decision log.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th scope="col">Decision</th><th scope="col">Choice</th><th scope="col">Tradeoff accepted</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Stock reservation</th><td>Atomic <code>UPDATE ... WHERE stock &gt;= n</code> plus a reservation row with an expiry</td><td>A worker must release expired holds. No row locks to reason about, and a <code>CHECK (stock &gt;= 0)</code> backstops it.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Background work</th><td>An outbox table in Postgres, drained by the worker (SQS was the original plan)</td><td>At-least-once delivery, so handlers must be idempotent. One event per transaction: fine for a shop, not a stream.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Authentication</th><td>Server-side sessions in the database, CSRF token on every write</td><td>The Next.js client must fetch a token first and forward cookies; an in-app proxy keeps them first-party.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Packaging</th><td>One jar, two profiles, two ECS services</td><td>A looser dependency graph than separate modules. Split only if it hurts.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Cloud networking</th><td>Fargate tasks in public subnets, no NAT gateway</td><td>Saves the NAT charge (not measured). The security group is then the only barrier, so I do not call it a private network.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </Section>

  <Section n={4} id="challenges" title="The three hardest problems">
    <h3>1. A payment webhook that must be safe to receive twice, late, or out of order</h3>
    <p>Stripe retries and reorders events, and a customer can pay after the order has expired. The order becomes paid only through the signed webhook, which does this:</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Verify the signature over the <em>raw</em> request bytes with a five-minute tolerance. A re-serialised body would not match. Bad, missing or stale signatures get a 400.</li>
      <li>In one transaction, insert the event ID with <code>ON CONFLICT DO NOTHING</code>. If nothing was inserted it is a repeat: answer 200 and stop.</li>
      <li>Lock the order row, check that the amount and currency match, move it to paid, record the payment, and commit the stock hold.</li>
      <li>If anything throws, roll back everything, including the event ID, and answer 500 so Stripe retries.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>The hard cases are the ones where the money cannot be honoured: a payment on an order that already expired, a wrong amount, or a stock hold that vanished. Those record the payment, queue a refund through the outbox, and issue it with a Stripe idempotency key. The order stays cancelled; I did not add a cancelled-to-refunded move, because a paid order is never cancelled.</p>
    <p>Tradeoff: the refund runs after commit, so for a short window (about one worker poll) the shopper is charged and not yet refunded.</p>

    <h3>2. Not overselling, without a deadlock</h3>
    <p>Reservation is one conditional <code>UPDATE ... WHERE stock &gt;= n</code> inside the checkout transaction, so a failure anywhere leaves no order and no hold. A per-customer advisory lock serialises one customer's checkouts and removes a duplicate-key race on the idempotency key.</p>
    <p>While adding the state machine I found that checkout's "cancel the old pending order" step released stock before cancelling, while a webhook paying the same order locked in the opposite order. That is a possible deadlock. I fixed it by fixing one lock order everywhere: the order row, then its reservations, then variant rows, and by cancelling before releasing stock.</p>

    <h3>3. A worker that can be killed at any moment</h3>
    <p>Events are written in the same transaction as the change that caused them, and the publish call refuses to run outside a transaction, so an event exists if and only if its change committed. The worker claims one event at a time with <code>FOR UPDATE SKIP LOCKED</code>, runs its handlers and marks it processed in one transaction. Kill the process and nothing commits, so the event is simply delivered again.</p>
    <p>A recorded limit: a kill is not counted as an attempt, so an event that crashes the whole JVM every time would retry forever. Nothing does that today; alarms on the age of the oldest pending event are the planned guard.</p>
  </Section>

  <Section n={5} id="evidence" title="Evidence">
    <p>Every number here has a row in the project's <code>docs/metrics.md</code>, and only rows marked <strong>Run</strong> (the output was seen) or <strong>User-reported</strong> (I ran it and recorded the result) are quoted. Each line says whether it was measured locally or on AWS. I ran none of these tests again while writing this page.</p>

    <h3>Correctness under failure (local)</h3>
    <FactList ids={['ssfWebhookDupes', 'ssfWebhookOrder', 'ssfStockRace', 'ssfWorkerKill', 'ssfStateMachine', 'ssfSuite']} />

    <h3>On AWS</h3>
    <FactList ids={['ssfCloudCheckout', 'ssfCloudChecks', 'ssfOutboxRetry', 'ssfTeardown', 'ssfStartup']} />

    <h3>Queries, frontend and accessibility (local)</h3>
    <FactList ids={['ssfExplain', 'ssfAxe', 'ssfPhotos', 'ssfLighthouse']} />

    <h3>Not measured yet</h3>
    <FactList ids={['ssfNotRun']} />

    <h3>Screenshots</h3>
    <p>Each image says what it was captured from. The brands and products are invented; the photos are licensed (CC0 or public domain) and credited on the store's credits page.</p>
    <Figure src={listing} alt="All shoes listing page showing 142 pairs, with a filter sidebar for occasion, who it is for, price, US size and availability, and a grid of shoe photos with brand, name, price and category." caption="Listing page against the real API running locally with the photographed demo catalogue. Filter counts come from the API." label="Real API data" crop="top" ratio="16 / 11" />
    <Figure src={pdp} alt="Product page for the Umber Chelsea Boot at $380: a large photo of a brown leather boot with two more thumbnails, US sizes 8 to 10.5, an Add to cart button and a More in Boots row." caption="Product page against the real API, with a three-photo gallery." label="Real API data" crop="top" ratio="16 / 11" />
    <Figure src={checkout} alt="Checkout page with a contact email field, a shipping address form, and an order summary card with three items, a $953 total and a Place order button." caption="Checkout screen. This capture is a design-review state on fixture data from before the photos were added; the sample email is a placeholder. The real checkout ran on AWS and is user-reported, not shown here." label="Fixture data" crop="top" ratio="16 / 11" />
    <Figure src={pdpPhone} alt="Phone-width product page for the Umber Chelsea Boot with a full-width photo, sizes, and an Add to cart button." caption="The same product page at phone width (390 px), real API data." label="Real API data" narrow crop="top" ratio="9 / 14" />
  </Section>

  <Section n={6} id="limits" title="Tradeoffs and limits">
    <ul>
      <li>The site is live, but every AWS number on this page comes from the earlier, destroyed sessions. The shop's order history page is still a design fixture, so the cloud purchase was confirmed from Stripe and the worker log, not from the shop.</li>
      <li>No load test and no measured cost per session. I make no claim about scale, throughput or latency under load. Lighthouse figures are local and simulated.</li>
      <li>Real photos made the listing's largest paint slower than the placeholders, even after the fix.</li>
      <li>Tasks have public IP addresses; the security group is the only barrier. The database connection encrypts but does not verify the server certificate.</li>
      <li>The database on AWS was Postgres 18.6 while tests ran on 18.7; the application's tests were not run against RDS.</li>
      <li>Sign-in has no rate limiting, password reset or email verification.</li>
      <li>Accessibility was checked with axe in Chromium only, on a mock API; no screen-reader walkthrough yet.</li>
      <li>All test results are my own records. Nobody else has reviewed them.</li>
    </ul>
  </Section>

  <Section n={7} id="next" title="What I would improve next">
    <p>All of this is planned, not done.</p>
    <ol>
      <li>Finish order history and the admin API, then wire the admin screens.</li>
      <li>Hardening: login rate limits, security headers, a dependency scan, and a load test whose setup is written down.</li>
      <li>Measure a session's real cost, replay webhooks against the cloud endpoint, and time a rebuild from zero.</li>
      <li>An alarm on the age of the oldest pending outbox event, and a metric for dead events.</li>
    </ol>
  </Section>

  <Section n={8} id="links" title="Links">
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Repository:</strong> <a href="https://github.com/jabrogena5100/RyuStoreFront" rel="noopener noreferrer">github.com/jabrogena5100/RyuStoreFront</a> (public). Its <code>docs/metrics.md</code> holds every number with its setup, date and status, and the decision log is in <code>docs/decisions.md</code>.</li>
      <li><strong>Live:</strong> <a href="https://ryustorefront.com" rel="noopener noreferrer">ryustorefront.com</a> (Stripe test mode: card 4242 4242 4242 4242, no card is charged)</li>
      <li><a href="/projects/">All projects</a></li>
    </ul>
  </Section>
</CaseStudy>
